The PR changes `ui-v2/tsconfig.app.json` from `"strict": true` to `"strict": false`. That turns off `strictNullChecks`, `noImplicitAny`, and the other strict family flags for every file under `src`, not just the four new components. The PR description does not mention it, and the new code does not need it. Once this is merged, type errors anywhere in the UI will stop failing the build, which weakens the type-safety guarantees for the whole automations area and beyond.

::diff{file="ui-v2/tsconfig.app.json" lines="16-22"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The base `tsconfig.node.json` still has `"strict": true`, so this change makes the two configs diverge without any stated reason.

If the new components produce type errors under strict mode, fix those errors locally in the components. Don't weaken the project-wide setting.

::suggestion{file="ui-v2/tsconfig.app.json" lines="19-19"}
		"strict": true,

`DeploymentStatusTriggerFields` picks `trigger.after` for Proactive and `trigger.expect` for Reactive. That matches `FlowRunStateTriggerFields`, which the PR says it is copying. `WorkPoolStatusTriggerFields` and `WorkQueueStatusTriggerFields` always write to `trigger.expect`, whatever the posture.

On the server, `after` and `expect` have different meanings for Proactive triggers. Proactive triggers respond to the absence of expected events. So a Proactive work pool status trigger built with the current code stores its status in the wrong key and behaves differently from a Proactive deployment status trigger with the same status. The inconsistency is easy to miss, because the Reactive case works the same in all three components.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/work-pool-status-trigger-fields.tsx" lines="33-40"}

::callout{type="warning"}
The work queue component has the same problem at its equivalent `trigger.expect` field, around line 36.

To fix it, add the same `statusFieldName` switch used in `deployment-status-trigger-fields.tsx` (lines 29-31), or better, pull the shared logic into one component (see the maintainability finding).

`PostureSelect` gives its trigger `aria-label="select posture"`. The new status selects don't set any label on `SelectTrigger`. The `FormLabel` "Status" points at the form control's id, but `FormControl` wraps a Radix `Select`, which doesn't render that id onto a DOM element. The result is a combobox that a screen reader announces with no name. The tests can't find it by label either, which is part of why the status select is untested.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/deployment-status-trigger-fields.tsx" lines="47-53"}

Add an `aria-label` to each status `SelectTrigger`, for example `aria-label="select status"`, matching the posture select.

`WorkPoolStatusTriggerFields` parses the threshold with `parseInt(e.target.value)`. The other components use `Number(e.target.value)`. `parseInt` without a radix works for these inputs, but the inconsistency is noise. Clearing the field produces `NaN`, which zod will reject with a confusing "expected number, received nan" message. `Number("")` gives `0`, which `min(1)` rejects with a clearer error.

::code{file="ui-v2/src/components/automations/automations-wizard/trigger-step/work-pool-status-trigger-fields.tsx" lines="76-80"}

Use `Number(e.target.value)` to match the other threshold fields.
